--TEST--
A `Core\Arr::shapeAs` subject missing an optional key leaves that field unwritten, while a nullable field whose value does not convert is `null` — `rule:core-api/required-optional-and-nullable`, `rule:types/shape-type` and `rule:expressions/nullable-conversion`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:core-api/required-optional-and-nullable`'s first column, written as
// `rule:types/shape-type`'s `?` marker: an absent optional key leaves the slot
// never written rather than holding `null`, so it encodes back out as an
// absent key instead of as `"s":null`.
echo Core\Json::encode(Core\Arr::shapeAs<{n: int, s?: string}>(["n" => 3])), "\n";

// The second column is the type's own, and `rule:expressions/nullable-conversion`
// answers it: a shape's `?T` field is `as ?T`, which yields `null` exactly
// where `as T` would have thrown. So a value the conversion refuses is that
// `null` rather than a reported issue.
echo Core\Json::encode(Core\Arr::shapeAs<{n: ?int}>(["n" => "not a number"])), "\n";

// The same field where the conversion succeeds is the converted value, which
// is what makes `as ?T` the checked form and not a weaker one.
echo Core\Json::encode(Core\Arr::shapeAs<{n: ?int}>(["n" => "7"])), "\n";

// The two questions stay separate: a *required* key is a failure however its
// type reads, and `{s: ?string}` demands an `s` that may hold `null`.
try {
    Core\Arr::shapeAs<{n: int, s: ?string}>(["n" => 1]);
    echo "not reached\n";
} catch (ParseError $missing) {
    echo "issues=", Core\Arr::count($missing->issues) as string, "\n";
    echo "  ", $missing->issues["0"]->path, ": ", $missing->issues["0"]->message, "\n";
}

// The absence is readable as absence and not only as an omitted key on the way
// back out: `rule:types/shape-type` names `??` and `isset` as the spellings
// that ask whether an optional field is there, and a hydrated shape answers
// them the same way an anonymous object that never wrote the key does. A
// `lateinit` property's unwritten slot still throws under the same guard
// (`rule:classes/an-unwritten-property-read-throws`) — one runtime state, two
// declarations, and only the shape's means "the subject did not carry it".
({n: int, s?: string}) $absent = Core\Arr::shapeAs<{n: int, s?: string}>(["n" => 4]);
echo $absent->s ?? "absent", "\n";
echo isset($absent->s) ? "set" : "unset", "\n";

// The same reads where the key did arrive, so the guard is answering presence
// rather than always taking its own arm.
({n: int, s?: string}) $carried = Core\Arr::shapeAs<{n: int, s?: string}>(["n" => 5, "s" => "here"]);
echo $carried->s ?? "absent", "\n";
echo isset($carried->s) ? "set" : "unset", "\n";
--EXPECT--
{"n":3}
{"n":null}
{"n":7}
issues=1
  s: required field missing
absent
unset
here
set
